; THE D-PAD IS THE WHOLE EQUIPMENT SCREEN, AND THERE IS NOTHING TO BIND.
;
; This is Elden Ring's D-pad minus the row this game has no content for (its ↑
; is the spell switch; ours is the loadout, which ER puts on a Y hold). LB IS
; DELIBERATELY EMPTY and is where spells go if they are ever built -- casting is
; a left-hand verb in every game this one borrows from, and leaving the button
; unbound is cheaper than binding it to something that then has to move:
;
;   ← / →   step the RIGHT HAND backwards / forwards through the weapons you
;           own: sword, bow, hookshot. The sword is a stop on that ring like
;           anything else, which is the answer to "how do I get my sword back"
;           and the reason there is no sheathe button any more.
;   ↓       step the selected ITEM -- the bottles. Selecting is not using.
;   ↑       swap the loadout SET, primary <-> alternate.
;
; X uses the selected item. RB and RT use whatever is in the right hand. Nothing
; on the D-pad fires anything, and that split is the point: Ocarina's C-button
; both equipped AND fired, so pulling the bow out was also loosing an arrow at
; whatever you happened to be facing.
;
; THE QUICK-SLOT BINDING SYSTEM IS GONE and this is what replaced it. Four
; bindable buttons, an ownership gate, an auto-bind on pickup and a whole card on
; the equipment screen existed so the player could decide WHICH DIRECTION the bow
; was on. A cycle has no such question, and the thing it costs -- direct access,
; two presses instead of one when you own three weapons -- is a cost the source
; material only avoids by having eight C-buttons' worth of screen furniture.
;
; ← is prev and → is next because that is the direction the icons move on screen,
; and the keyboard row is 1 2 3 4 reading around the pad the same way.
